When SpringSaLaD is the right tool. Ask for it when the answer depends on where things are:
when excluded volume matters - crowding, or a site shielded by the rest of its own molecule;
when the tether matters - a kinase that can only reach a substrate it is anchored near;
when the question is about complexes rather than concentrations - cluster sizes, how many molecules end up in the largest aggregate, how that changes with valency;
when the membrane is doing real work - a receptor whose two halves live on opposite sides of it.
When it is not. Every particle is tracked individually and every pair is tested for collision, so cost grows quickly with particle number. A model of tens of thousands of molecules, or one that has to run for minutes of simulated time, belongs in a network-free or deterministic application instead. Nothing is lost by trying both: they are two applications over the same physiology.
Where to start. Build the molecules and rules as you would for any rule-based model, then create a SpringSaLaD application over them. It supplies the geometry itself, and its Molecular Structures tab is where the spatial detail goes.
